            <ArticleTitle>Macrophage migration inhibitory factor in obese and non obese women with polycystic ovary syndrome.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>31-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.endonu.2014.09.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1575-0922(14)00251-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To measure macrophage migration inhibitory factor (MIF) concentrations in obese and non-obese women diagnosed with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Women diagnosed with PCOS and age-matched healthy controls with regular menses and normal ovaries on ultrasound examination were selected and divided into 4 groups (group A, PCOS and obese; group B, PCOS and non-obese; group C, obese controls; and group D, non-obese controls) based on body mass index (obese &gt;30 kg/m2 and non-obese &lt;25 kg/m2). Luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, androstenedione, testosterone, sex hormone-binding globulin, serum glucose, insulin and MIF levels were measured.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Obese and non-obese women with PCOS had higher luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, androstenedione, testosterone, and insulin levels as compared to the obese and non-obese control groups, respectively (P &lt; .0001). Women with PCOS had significantly higher MIF levels (group A, 48.6 ± 9.9 mg/ml; group B, 35.2 ± 6.0 ng/ml) as compared to controls (group C, 13.5 ± 6.0 ng/ml; group D, 12.0 ± 4.3 ng/dl; P &lt; .0001). A weak, positive and significant correlation was seen between fasting blood glucose and insulin levels in women with PCOS (P &lt; .05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Significant differences exist in plasma MIF levels between obese and non-obese women with and without PCOS.</AbstractText>
